<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/0235b8e3-1d04-4ce5-9e05-242a3e58fc9d_2> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:HistoricalEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Unknown"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The third inscription on the site, located on the pedestal of five donor statues carved on a single stone, is also in poor condition. As a result, the full details of the inscription cannot be discerned due to letter breakage. However, names such as Gopīdeva, the primary donor of the shrine, along with others like Śaṅkaradeva (also written as Saṃkharadeva) and Vīradeva (also written as Viradeva), can still be identified. This confirms that the statues belong to the donor family. The precise event recorded by this undated inscription remains unclear. It is likely that Gopīdeva commissioned these statues for himself and his family when he established the shrine back in 1663 CE (NS 796)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0235b8e3-1d04-4ce5-9e05-242a3e58fc9d> ;
    rdfs:comment "The third inscription on the site, located on the pedestal of five donor statues carved on a single stone, is also in poor condition. As a result, the full details of the inscription cannot be discerned due to letter breakage. However, names such as Gopīdeva, the primary donor of the shrine, along with others like Śaṅkaradeva (also written as Saṃkharadeva) and Vīradeva (also written as Viradeva), can still be identified. This confirms that the statues belong to the donor family. The precise event recorded by this undated inscription remains unclear. It is likely that Gopīdeva commissioned these statues for himself and his family when he established the shrine back in 1663 CE (NS 796)."@en ;
