<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724_0> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Four stone inscriptions are engraved on the façade of the temple. In addition to these, inscriptions are also found on the stone surfaces used around the middle tier of the temple, which contain references related to its construction. However, it remains unclear which of these inscriptions specifically pertains to the temple’s construction. This uncertainty arises because the central portions of the inscriptions—where dates would typically be found—are difficult to read due to repeated annual whitewashing and erosion. According to Pruscha (1975, p. 202), the Dyaḥnani temple was constructed slightly earlier, around 1617 CE (NS 737–38) by Sanjajena Śākya, though no primary source is cited."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724> ;
    rdfs:comment "Four stone inscriptions are engraved on the façade of the temple. In addition to these, inscriptions are also found on the stone surfaces used around the middle tier of the temple, which contain references related to its construction. However, it remains unclear which of these inscriptions specifically pertains to the temple’s construction. This uncertainty arises because the central portions of the inscriptions—where dates would typically be found—are difficult to read due to repeated annual whitewashing and erosion. According to Pruscha (1975, p. 202), the Dyaḥnani temple was constructed slightly earlier, around 1617 CE (NS 737–38) by Sanjajena Śākya, though no primary source is cited."@en ;
