<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724_1> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Formation of guṭhī"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Among the inscriptions found here, the only one with a legible date is from 1664 CE (NS 784), which records the establishment of aguṭhī(Pruscha 1975, p. 202)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724> ;
    rdfs:comment "Among the inscriptions found here, the only one with a legible date is from 1664 CE (NS 784), which records the establishment of aguṭhī(Pruscha 1975, p. 202)."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
    hg:eventIndex 1 ;
    hg:extractionMethod "source_table" ;
    hg:isEventOf <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724> .

<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724_2> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Unclear event"@en ;
    dcterms:description "On either side of the western entrance of the temple are four stone slabs bearing inscriptions. One of these is carved on the same pale-yellow stone used in the temple’s construction, suggesting that it dates back to the temple’s original building phase. However, due to lime plastering and erosion, the text is difficult to read, and the date is no longer visible. The inscription lists ritual duties to be performed at the temple and names the responsible individuals."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724> ;
    rdfs:comment "On either side of the western entrance of the temple are four stone slabs bearing inscriptions. One of these is carved on the same pale-yellow stone used in the temple’s construction, suggesting that it dates back to the temple’s original building phase. However, due to lime plastering and erosion, the text is difficult to read, and the date is no longer visible. The inscription lists ritual duties to be performed at the temple and names the responsible individuals."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
    hg:eventIndex 2 ;
    hg:extractionMethod "source_table" ;
    hg:isEventOf <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724> .

<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724_3> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Direction for rituals and land endowment"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Another of the four inscriptions on the façade also has an eroded date, though its script indicates it belongs to the late medieval period. It mentions that offerings and meals should be provided to the goddess Cakreśvarī of Ināyalã, to Manideva of Cakrabāhāḥ, and to representatives of Haḥbāhāḥ and Bhinchẽbāhāḥ. It also states that rice-filledpiṇḍapātrasshould be donated to Buṅgadyah and the monastic community of Kwābāhāḥ. In addition, the inscription details ritual items to be offered during the temple’s annual festival (varṣavardhana) and on days of special observances (possiblysaṃvaradiśi(?). The location and measurements of land allocated to finance these activities are also given, though the donor’s name and date are no longer readable."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/0274d17a-ceb6-4480-bcbd-8d893d078724> ;
    rdfs:comment "Another of the four inscriptions on the façade also has an eroded date, though its script indicates it belongs to the late medieval period. It mentions that offerings and meals should be provided to the goddess Cakreśvarī of Ināyalã, to Manideva of Cakrabāhāḥ, and to representatives of Haḥbāhāḥ and Bhinchẽbāhāḥ. It also states that rice-filledpiṇḍapātrasshould be donated to Buṅgadyah and the monastic community of Kwābāhāḥ. In addition, the inscription details ritual items to be offered during the temple’s annual festival (varṣavardhana) and on days of special observances (possiblysaṃvaradiśi(?). The location and measurements of land allocated to finance these activities are also given, though the donor’s name and date are no longer readable."@en ;
