<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/17c3869a-7006-4c37-8f3e-6bac4c6ba067_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Construction of the reservoir"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The year of construction of the reservoir is known from the inscription engraved on the door lintel. As for most inscriptions of the time, the main body of text is written in a Nāgarī script, preceeded by the mantra of Avalokiteśvara (oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ) written in the Rañjana script. The same is observed on the two-sided inscription of the Kīrtikhambā (DLK0260-001).The text of the inscription, published by Giuseppe Tucci (1956, p. 45), is as follow:Oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ(Rañjana script)1.oṃ svasti śrīśāke 1276 pauṣe vadi 2 śukravāśare mahārājādhirājaparameśvaraparamabhaṭṭārakaparamadhārmi[ka]2.paramakalpadrumāvatāraśrīmatpṛthvīmalladevavijayarājye|mahāmatyaśrīyaśovarmadevānāṃ samaye||3.naṣṭā śrīr bhi(sic)marājasya dhararājasya dhīmataḥ|cchatyālavaṃśatilakaḥ sūnur graṃthadvavarmaṇaḥ||nāvalladevyā saṃjāto||4.devavarmā mahāmatiḥ|tenāpūrvatarā vāpī nirmitā amaravallabhā||caityapaṃcakasaṃyuktā vasati[r]buddhasaṃtateḥ|vi5.śrāmabhūmi śrāntānāṃ jīvitaṃ sarvadehināṃ mahāmatya śrīmaddevavarmadevaiḥ sarvasattvopakārārthaṃ svakīyokta6.budhyā(sic)nūtanarītyā | devānām api vallabhā | vāpī viracitā likhita dharmadāsena | iti subham astuThe translation published by Mahes Raj Pant (2009, p. 301-302), goes as follows:śrīśāke 1276 pauṣe vadi 2 śukravāśare mahārājādhirājaparameśvaraparamabhaṭṭārakaparamadhārmikaparamakalpadrumāvatāraśrīmatpṛthvīmalladevavijayarājye |mahāmatyaśrīmatyaśovarmadevānāṃ samaye ||naptā śrīr bhimarājasya dhararājasya dhīmataḥ |chatyālavaṃśatilakaḥ sūnur graṃthadvavarmaṇaḥ ||nāvalladevyāḥ saṃjāto || devavarmā mahāmatiḥ |tenāpūrvatarā vāpī nirmitā amaravallabhā ||caityapaṃcakasaṃyuktā vasati buddhasaṃtateḥ |viśrāmabhūmi śrāntānāṃ jīvitaṃ sarvadehināṃ mahāmatyaśrīmaddevavarmadevaiḥ sarvasattvopakārārātha svakīyāktabudhyā nūtanarītyā | devānām api vallabhā | vāpī viracitā ||Translation:In the glorious [year of] Sake 1276, in Pauṣa, in the waning moon, on the 2nd, on Friday; in the realm of victory of the illustrious Pṛthvīmalladeva, who is the supreme king of great kings, the supreme lord, the supreme king and a supremely pious [man] (paramadhārmika), the supreme incarnation of the Wishing Tree; during the time of the great minister (mahāmātya) the illustriousYasovarmadeva-he who is the great-minded (mahāmati) Devavarmā, the ornament (tilaka) of the Chatyāla family, the grandson of glorious Bhīmarāja [and] the wise Dhanarāja, the son of the Varmā named Grantha, and born of Navalladevī, built an unprecedented water reservoir (vāpī) which is dear to the gods, is endowed with fivecaitya-s and is a residence for the progeny of the Buddha, a place (bhumī) to rest for those who are weary, and life to all living creatures. •The Great Minister the illustrious Devavarmadeva built a water reservoir, dear even to the gods, for the benefit of all beings in accordance with his own intention, as stated [above], using a new method."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/17c3869a-7006-4c37-8f3e-6bac4c6ba067> ;
    rdfs:comment "The year of construction of the reservoir is known from the inscription engraved on the door lintel. As for most inscriptions of the time, the main body of text is written in a Nāgarī script, preceeded by the mantra of Avalokiteśvara (oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ) written in the Rañjana script. The same is observed on the two-sided inscription of the Kīrtikhambā (DLK0260-001).The text of the inscription, published by Giuseppe Tucci (1956, p. 45), is as follow:Oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ(Rañjana script)1.oṃ svasti śrīśāke 1276 pauṣe vadi 2 śukravāśare mahārājādhirājaparameśvaraparamabhaṭṭārakaparamadhārmi[ka]2.paramakalpadrumāvatāraśrīmatpṛthvīmalladevavijayarājye|mahāmatyaśrīyaśovarmadevānāṃ samaye||3.naṣṭā śrīr bhi(sic)marājasya dhararājasya dhīmataḥ|cchatyālavaṃśatilakaḥ sūnur graṃthadvavarmaṇaḥ||nāvalladevyā saṃjāto||4.devavarmā mahāmatiḥ|tenāpūrvatarā vāpī nirmitā amaravallabhā||caityapaṃcakasaṃyuktā vasati[r]buddhasaṃtateḥ|vi5.śrāmabhūmi śrāntānāṃ jīvitaṃ sarvadehināṃ mahāmatya śrīmaddevavarmadevaiḥ sarvasattvopakārārthaṃ svakīyokta6.budhyā(sic)nūtanarītyā | devānām api vallabhā | vāpī viracitā likhita dharmadāsena | iti subham astuThe translation published by Mahes Raj Pant (2009, p. 301-302), goes as follows:śrīśāke 1276 pauṣe vadi 2 śukravāśare mahārājādhirājaparameśvaraparamabhaṭṭārakaparamadhārmikaparamakalpadrumāvatāraśrīmatpṛthvīmalladevavijayarājye |mahāmatyaśrīmatyaśovarmadevānāṃ samaye ||naptā śrīr bhimarājasya dhararājasya dhīmataḥ |chatyālavaṃśatilakaḥ sūnur graṃthadvavarmaṇaḥ ||nāvalladevyāḥ saṃjāto || devavarmā mahāmatiḥ |tenāpūrvatarā vāpī nirmitā amaravallabhā ||caityapaṃcakasaṃyuktā vasati buddhasaṃtateḥ |viśrāmabhūmi śrāntānāṃ jīvitaṃ sarvadehināṃ mahāmatyaśrīmaddevavarmadevaiḥ sarvasattvopakārārātha svakīyāktabudhyā nūtanarītyā | devānām api vallabhā | vāpī viracitā ||Translation:In the glorious [year of] Sake 1276, in Pauṣa, in the waning moon, on the 2nd, on Friday; in the realm of victory of the illustrious Pṛthvīmalladeva, who is the supreme king of great kings, the supreme lord, the supreme king and a supremely pious [man] (paramadhārmika), the supreme incarnation of the Wishing Tree; during the time of the great minister (mahāmātya) the illustriousYasovarmadeva-he who is the great-minded (mahāmati) Devavarmā, the ornament (tilaka) of the Chatyāla family, the grandson of glorious Bhīmarāja [and] the wise Dhanarāja, the son of the Varmā named Grantha, and born of Navalladevī, built an unprecedented water reservoir (vāpī) which is dear to the gods, is endowed with fivecaitya-s and is a residence for the progeny of the Buddha, a place (bhumī) to rest for those who are weary, and life to all living creatures. •The Great Minister the illustrious Devavarmadeva built a water reservoir, dear even to the gods, for the benefit of all beings in accordance with his own intention, as stated [above], using a new method."@en ;
