<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/1e598519-a2aa-4ea7-83bf-3b4f141b4186_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Enshrinement ;
    rdfs:label "Construction and Maintenance of a Hiti and Phalcā at Thimi"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The earliest inscription found at the site is a stone inscription dated to CE 1732 (NS 852). It is not related to the construction of the Sataḥ but instead pertains to Thimi. This is evident from the depiction of Bālakumārī and the inscriptional reference to “Śrī Śrī Śrī Bārakumāli (the venerable goddess Bālakumārī).” The text records that residents of Codo ṭola in Thimi collectively built ahiti(stone water spout) and aphalcānear the pond where Kvarakhu Bālakumārīdevī is enshrined, assigning maintenance responsibilities to local inhabitants under threat ofpañcamahāpātaka(the five grievous sins) for neglect."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/1e598519-a2aa-4ea7-83bf-3b4f141b4186> ;
    rdfs:comment "The earliest inscription found at the site is a stone inscription dated to CE 1732 (NS 852). It is not related to the construction of the Sataḥ but instead pertains to Thimi. This is evident from the depiction of Bālakumārī and the inscriptional reference to “Śrī Śrī Śrī Bārakumāli (the venerable goddess Bālakumārī).” The text records that residents of Codo ṭola in Thimi collectively built ahiti(stone water spout) and aphalcānear the pond where Kvarakhu Bālakumārīdevī is enshrined, assigning maintenance responsibilities to local inhabitants under threat ofpañcamahāpātaka(the five grievous sins) for neglect."@en ;
