<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/20c0e0a0-2b7f-491a-81aa-41dddec3276b_1> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Retaining of trust lands"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Prime Minister Jaṅga Bahādura Rāṇā retained the 40murī-land trust that Pṛthvīnārāyaṇa Śāha had granted for the Śyāmapāṭī Śiva temple. He issued a royal decree (lālamohara) in the names of Colonel Gajarāja Siṃha Thāpā, Lieutenant Dalabhajana Thāpā, and Major Captain Keśara Siṃha Thāpā to retain the trust. Among the three persons mentioned in thelālamohara, Gajarāja Siṃha Thāpā seems to have familial ties with Jaṅga Bahādura. One of Jaṅga Bahādura’s daughters, Badana Kumārī (Magarnī Maiyā̃), was married to Gajarāja, who started tea cultivation in Ilam. Since it is mentioned as “62sāla” in thelālamohāra, it is possible that the land of Śyāmapāṭī Śiva temple was also confiscated by Raṇa Bahādura Śāha in 1805 CE (VS 1862), and Jaṅga Bahādura may have returned the same land to retain the trust."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/20c0e0a0-2b7f-491a-81aa-41dddec3276b> ;
    rdfs:comment "Prime Minister Jaṅga Bahādura Rāṇā retained the 40murī-land trust that Pṛthvīnārāyaṇa Śāha had granted for the Śyāmapāṭī Śiva temple. He issued a royal decree (lālamohara) in the names of Colonel Gajarāja Siṃha Thāpā, Lieutenant Dalabhajana Thāpā, and Major Captain Keśara Siṃha Thāpā to retain the trust. Among the three persons mentioned in thelālamohara, Gajarāja Siṃha Thāpā seems to have familial ties with Jaṅga Bahādura. One of Jaṅga Bahādura’s daughters, Badana Kumārī (Magarnī Maiyā̃), was married to Gajarāja, who started tea cultivation in Ilam. Since it is mentioned as “62sāla” in thelālamohāra, it is possible that the land of Śyāmapāṭī Śiva temple was also confiscated by Raṇa Bahādura Śāha in 1805 CE (VS 1862), and Jaṅga Bahādura may have returned the same land to retain the trust."@en ;
