<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/227169da-a4d2-4266-bbbd-1a3f4a1dd5eb_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "A Licchavi Royal Edict of tax exemption and local jurisdiction establishment"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Although the inscription associated with this event is unrelated to the Mañjuśrī and Nāsaḥdyaḥ Dyaḥchẽ, it substantiates the Licchavi settlement status of the area. Aṃśuvarman (r. 605-621 CE) erected this inscription, featuring a royal edict on behalf of the Vatu ṭola populace. In the proclamation, he granted a tax exemption for sheep and pig farming, previously levied on the Vatu ṭola community. Additionally, he empowered the local chief administrator (pāñcālī) to oversee cases involving the five major crimes (pañcāparādha)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/227169da-a4d2-4266-bbbd-1a3f4a1dd5eb> ;
    rdfs:comment "Although the inscription associated with this event is unrelated to the Mañjuśrī and Nāsaḥdyaḥ Dyaḥchẽ, it substantiates the Licchavi settlement status of the area. Aṃśuvarman (r. 605-621 CE) erected this inscription, featuring a royal edict on behalf of the Vatu ṭola populace. In the proclamation, he granted a tax exemption for sheep and pig farming, previously levied on the Vatu ṭola community. Additionally, he empowered the local chief administrator (pāñcālī) to oversee cases involving the five major crimes (pañcāparādha)."@en ;
