<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/3eed6ff1-e60f-4425-b7dc-052f8094f611_7> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:RitualEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Addition of Bhairava mask"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Various inscriptions from Kirtipur provide historical evidence of the hāthu hayke tradition, where fermented beer (thvã) is discharged from a Bhairava mask's mouth for people to drink. These inscriptions include one from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823), all of which mention thehāthu hāyketradition.In more recent times, the Sun Star Club, a youth organization in Kirtipur, aimed to revive this tradition after the reconstruction of the Phalcā and began planning for it in 2019 CE. Krishna Prasad Maharjan and Arjun Prasad Maharjan, the sons of the late Prem Bahadur Maharjan and Ganesh Kumari Maharjan, provided the generous funding of the crafting the Bhairava mask. The responsibility for carving the mask was entrusted to Laxman Maharjan, one of the ost prominent skilled wood craftsmen from Bungamati. However, Laxman Maharjan passed away due to the coronavirus before finalising the mask. His students, namely Rambhakta Maharjan, Amir Shakya, and Jitendra Tuladhar, took over the carving of the Bhairava mask, which stands at over 5 feet tall, and is thus, as of now, the largest Bhairava mask in Kathmandu Valley.On March 1, 2022, the Bhairava mask was transported from the craftsmen's workshop in Bungamati to Kirtipur. The mask's journey was accompanied by a traditional musical ensemble and a celebratory procession, and it was ultimately installed at the Phalcā as part of the hāthu hāyke tradition's revival.Various inscriptions from Kirtipur reveal the existence of thehāthuhayketradition when fermented beer (thvã) is discharged from a Bhairava mask’s mouth for the people to drink: An inscription from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another inscription from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another one from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823) are some inscriptions that mention thehāthuhāyketradition. The Sun Star Club, an organization run by local youths, had aimed to restart the tradition after the reconstruction of the Phalcā and began planning in 2019 CE. Krishna Prasad Maharjan and Arjun Prasad Maharjan, sons of the late Prem Bahadur Maharjan and Ganesh Kumari Maharjan, agreed to provide the funds for carving the large Bhairava mask. Senior wood craftsman from Bungamati Laxman Maharjan was given the responsibility to carve the mask. Unfortunately, he passed away due to the coronavirus. Then, his students Rambhakta Maharjan, Amir Shakya, and Jitendra Tuladhar completed the carving of over 5-feet tall Bhairava mask. On March 1, 2022, the Bhairava mask was brought from the craftsmen’s workshop in Bungamati to Kirtipur in the company of the traditional musical ensemble and pageantry to be installed at the Phalcā.Various inscriptions from Kirtipur provide historical evidence of the hāthu hayke tradition, where fermented beer (thvã) is discharged from a Bhairava mask's mouth for people to drink. These inscriptions include one from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823), all of which mention thehāthu hāyketradition.In more recent times, the Sun Star Club, a youth organization in Kirtipur, aimed to revive this tradition after the reconstruction of the Phalcā and began planning for it in 2019 CE. Krishna Prasad Maharjan and Arjun Prasad Maharjan, the sons of the late Prem Bahadur Maharjan and Ganesh Kumari Maharjan, generously agreed to cover the expenses of crafting the Bhairava mask.The responsibility for carving the mask was entrusted to a skilled wood craftsman from Bungamati, Laxman Maharjan. Unfortunately, Laxman Maharjan passed away due to the coronavirus. Subsequently, his students, namely Rambhakta Maharjan, Amir Shakya, and Jitendra Tuladhar, took over the carving of the Bhairava mask, which stands at over 5 feet tall.On March 1, 2022, the Bhairava mask was transported from the craftsmen's workshop in Bungamati to Kirtipur. The mask's journey was accompanied by a traditional musical ensemble and a celebratory procession, and it was ultimately installed at the Phalcā as part of the hāthu hāyke tradition's revival.[1]An inscription from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another inscription from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another one from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823) are some inscriptions that talk about thehāthuhāyketradition."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/3eed6ff1-e60f-4425-b7dc-052f8094f611> ;
    rdfs:comment "Various inscriptions from Kirtipur provide historical evidence of the hāthu hayke tradition, where fermented beer (thvã) is discharged from a Bhairava mask's mouth for people to drink. These inscriptions include one from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823), all of which mention thehāthu hāyketradition.In more recent times, the Sun Star Club, a youth organization in Kirtipur, aimed to revive this tradition after the reconstruction of the Phalcā and began planning for it in 2019 CE. Krishna Prasad Maharjan and Arjun Prasad Maharjan, the sons of the late Prem Bahadur Maharjan and Ganesh Kumari Maharjan, provided the generous funding of the crafting the Bhairava mask. The responsibility for carving the mask was entrusted to Laxman Maharjan, one of the ost prominent skilled wood craftsmen from Bungamati. However, Laxman Maharjan passed away due to the coronavirus before finalising the mask. His students, namely Rambhakta Maharjan, Amir Shakya, and Jitendra Tuladhar, took over the carving of the Bhairava mask, which stands at over 5 feet tall, and is thus, as of now, the largest Bhairava mask in Kathmandu Valley.On March 1, 2022, the Bhairava mask was transported from the craftsmen's workshop in Bungamati to Kirtipur. The mask's journey was accompanied by a traditional musical ensemble and a celebratory procession, and it was ultimately installed at the Phalcā as part of the hāthu hāyke tradition's revival.Various inscriptions from Kirtipur reveal the existence of thehāthuhayketradition when fermented beer (thvã) is discharged from a Bhairava mask’s mouth for the people to drink: An inscription from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another inscription from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another one from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823) are some inscriptions that mention thehāthuhāyketradition. The Sun Star Club, an organization run by local youths, had aimed to restart the tradition after the reconstruction of the Phalcā and began planning in 2019 CE. Krishna Prasad Maharjan and Arjun Prasad Maharjan, sons of the late Prem Bahadur Maharjan and Ganesh Kumari Maharjan, agreed to provide the funds for carving the large Bhairava mask. Senior wood craftsman from Bungamati Laxman Maharjan was given the responsibility to carve the mask. Unfortunately, he passed away due to the coronavirus. Then, his students Rambhakta Maharjan, Amir Shakya, and Jitendra Tuladhar completed the carving of over 5-feet tall Bhairava mask. On March 1, 2022, the Bhairava mask was brought from the craftsmen’s workshop in Bungamati to Kirtipur in the company of the traditional musical ensemble and pageantry to be installed at the Phalcā.Various inscriptions from Kirtipur provide historical evidence of the hāthu hayke tradition, where fermented beer (thvã) is discharged from a Bhairava mask's mouth for people to drink. These inscriptions include one from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823), all of which mention thehāthu hāyketradition.In more recent times, the Sun Star Club, a youth organization in Kirtipur, aimed to revive this tradition after the reconstruction of the Phalcā and began planning for it in 2019 CE. Krishna Prasad Maharjan and Arjun Prasad Maharjan, the sons of the late Prem Bahadur Maharjan and Ganesh Kumari Maharjan, generously agreed to cover the expenses of crafting the Bhairava mask.The responsibility for carving the mask was entrusted to a skilled wood craftsman from Bungamati, Laxman Maharjan. Unfortunately, Laxman Maharjan passed away due to the coronavirus. Subsequently, his students, namely Rambhakta Maharjan, Amir Shakya, and Jitendra Tuladhar, took over the carving of the Bhairava mask, which stands at over 5 feet tall.On March 1, 2022, the Bhairava mask was transported from the craftsmen's workshop in Bungamati to Kirtipur. The mask's journey was accompanied by a traditional musical ensemble and a celebratory procession, and it was ultimately installed at the Phalcā as part of the hāthu hāyke tradition's revival.[1]An inscription from 1694 CE (NS 814) installed at the Khyālidyaḥ Shrine in Kvāḥcva, another inscription from 1698 CE (NS 818) at the Ichẽ Ṭola, another one from 1693 CE (NS 813), and a copperplate inscription from 1703 CE (NS 823) are some inscriptions that talk about thehāthuhāyketradition."@en ;
