<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/43291104-1bbd-11ea-b9b1-0242ac130002_1> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Restoration 2002"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The Cābāhāḥ Phalcā was restored on the basis of a plan designed by the Urban Management and Economic Diversification Project (UMEDP), the Kathmandu Valley Preservation Trust (KVPT) and Jyāpu Samāja, Lalitpur. The final report was written up by Rohit Ranjitkar and Lumanti Joshi in March 2003.Six weeks of research went into the reconstruction process, where researchers tried to work out what the Cābāhāḥ Phalcā may once have looked like (its earlier forms had not been documented). Four different options for the front facade were developed and then shared with the community elders, who were ultimately responsible for the final decision. The window designs were copied from similarphalcādating back to the same period in the Chochhe Gallī area.Emphasis was given to the use of traditional building materials and craftsmanship, something which was uncommon back then.The reconstruction was also an attempt to challenge common views that traditional materials are not sustainable; those who argue this often propose cement be used instead. The process also included training workshops for and by traditional craftsmanship. Two new pillars were recarved on the basis of the historical model. Because it was in such bad shape, a layer of bricks adjacent to the residential house to the west was removed and replaced withma apāusing yellow mud mortar. The niches were installed in line with the photographs taken prior to the dismantling of the rear wall, and the stone statues of the guardian deities Gaṇeśa, Lokeśvara Kumāra, Umāmaheśvara and two others of Cābahā Phalcā were stored there. These statues have been carved anew. Some innovative measures were also introduced, such as putting a waterproof membrane between the planks and the mud bed for the roof tiles."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/43291104-1bbd-11ea-b9b1-0242ac130002> ;
    rdfs:comment "The Cābāhāḥ Phalcā was restored on the basis of a plan designed by the Urban Management and Economic Diversification Project (UMEDP), the Kathmandu Valley Preservation Trust (KVPT) and Jyāpu Samāja, Lalitpur. The final report was written up by Rohit Ranjitkar and Lumanti Joshi in March 2003.Six weeks of research went into the reconstruction process, where researchers tried to work out what the Cābāhāḥ Phalcā may once have looked like (its earlier forms had not been documented). Four different options for the front facade were developed and then shared with the community elders, who were ultimately responsible for the final decision. The window designs were copied from similarphalcādating back to the same period in the Chochhe Gallī area.Emphasis was given to the use of traditional building materials and craftsmanship, something which was uncommon back then.The reconstruction was also an attempt to challenge common views that traditional materials are not sustainable; those who argue this often propose cement be used instead. The process also included training workshops for and by traditional craftsmanship. Two new pillars were recarved on the basis of the historical model. Because it was in such bad shape, a layer of bricks adjacent to the residential house to the west was removed and replaced withma apāusing yellow mud mortar. The niches were installed in line with the photographs taken prior to the dismantling of the rear wall, and the stone statues of the guardian deities Gaṇeśa, Lokeśvara Kumāra, Umāmaheśvara and two others of Cābahā Phalcā were stored there. These statues have been carved anew. Some innovative measures were also introduced, such as putting a waterproof membrane between the planks and the mud bed for the roof tiles."@en ;
