<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/4abcfc57-87bc-46d0-9338-add4863dbf46_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Politically motivated damage of the Phalcā"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The Phalcā was damaged in 1979, and since then has been turned into an elevated open space.This important Phalcā was located in the heart of Sundhara Patan. It was occupied by a local police station before it was vandalised in 1978-9. The youths of Nugaḥ Ṭola used to hold regular gang fights with the youths from neighbouring quarters in this area. One such fight took place in 1978-9. The local police arrested the youths involved in the gang fight and detained them in the Phalcā building itself An aggressive group of local people attacked the police office, demanding the release of the detainees. After this incident, the local police station was moved to another Phalcā located to the west of Lũ Hiti. Pvāḥsyāḥ Phalcā was abandoned completely and was no longer maintained (source: a resident of Yanāmugaḥ, male, age 45). Though the Phalcā had aguṭhī(established by Cipālus (Śreṣṭha) of Caṃki Ṭola), this group no longer exists today. The idol of Pvāḥsyāḥdyaḥ is also no longer present; it appears that the statue of this deity was stolen and never replaced."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/4abcfc57-87bc-46d0-9338-add4863dbf46> ;
    rdfs:comment "The Phalcā was damaged in 1979, and since then has been turned into an elevated open space.This important Phalcā was located in the heart of Sundhara Patan. It was occupied by a local police station before it was vandalised in 1978-9. The youths of Nugaḥ Ṭola used to hold regular gang fights with the youths from neighbouring quarters in this area. One such fight took place in 1978-9. The local police arrested the youths involved in the gang fight and detained them in the Phalcā building itself An aggressive group of local people attacked the police office, demanding the release of the detainees. After this incident, the local police station was moved to another Phalcā located to the west of Lũ Hiti. Pvāḥsyāḥ Phalcā was abandoned completely and was no longer maintained (source: a resident of Yanāmugaḥ, male, age 45). Though the Phalcā had aguṭhī(established by Cipālus (Śreṣṭha) of Caṃki Ṭola), this group no longer exists today. The idol of Pvāḥsyāḥdyaḥ is also no longer present; it appears that the statue of this deity was stolen and never replaced."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
