<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/5d7b848b-687f-4ac3-b424-71c5e107e1b5_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Thisjaḥdhuṃ(water trough) was originally constructed by the main road connecting Cāpāgāũ and Theco, serving as a vital source of water for travelers and passersby. While the original inscription is no longer present, its text was published by D. Vajrācārya (VS 2056, p. 298). The inscription reveals that in 1697 CE (NS 818), four brothers—Lvaṃvā Bhāro, Vāsiṃha Bhāro, Pabhārāma Bhāro, and Devarāma Bhāro—from Capāla Livichẽ, Cāpāgāũ, built aphalcā(rest house). They dedicated a statue of Madhusudana in their father's name and installed statues of three deities in honor of their mothers, Sarasvatī Mayī and Maṇikarṇṇa Mayī. Concurrently, ajaḥdhuṃwas also established in the names of their parents.To ensure the upkeep of these structures and the continuation of cultural rituals, land was endowed as a trust. The inscription mandated various annual ceremonies, including ancestral worship (śrāddha), communal meals for Brahmin couples, Jośīs (astrologers), andācāryas (teachers), and other forms of worship. Furthermore, the trust was obligated to provide 67pāthīs of rice annually to the individual responsible for consistently refilling thejaḥdhuṃ's water trough."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/5d7b848b-687f-4ac3-b424-71c5e107e1b5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Thisjaḥdhuṃ(water trough) was originally constructed by the main road connecting Cāpāgāũ and Theco, serving as a vital source of water for travelers and passersby. While the original inscription is no longer present, its text was published by D. Vajrācārya (VS 2056, p. 298). The inscription reveals that in 1697 CE (NS 818), four brothers—Lvaṃvā Bhāro, Vāsiṃha Bhāro, Pabhārāma Bhāro, and Devarāma Bhāro—from Capāla Livichẽ, Cāpāgāũ, built aphalcā(rest house). They dedicated a statue of Madhusudana in their father's name and installed statues of three deities in honor of their mothers, Sarasvatī Mayī and Maṇikarṇṇa Mayī. Concurrently, ajaḥdhuṃwas also established in the names of their parents.To ensure the upkeep of these structures and the continuation of cultural rituals, land was endowed as a trust. The inscription mandated various annual ceremonies, including ancestral worship (śrāddha), communal meals for Brahmin couples, Jośīs (astrologers), andācāryas (teachers), and other forms of worship. Furthermore, the trust was obligated to provide 67pāthīs of rice annually to the individual responsible for consistently refilling thejaḥdhuṃ's water trough."@en ;
