<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/65867863-c05d-4590-bfbc-4ea0bcfd1339_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction of hiti"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Amara Siṃha Kumhara and his wife Kīrttimaṅgalā constructed the water spout (hiti) and aphalcā(public shelter) in memory of their deceased son. They also set up a trust supported by land endowments of 2.5ropanīs in Pāko and 5ropanīs in Aphala for their upkeep. The income from these land parcels was used to clear grass from thehitiandphalcāon the 1st solar day of Vaiśākha and to distributesamaybaji, a Newar food set including beaten rice, puffed rice, roasted meat balls, peas, soybeans, etc. Kīrttimaṅgalā was the second daughter of King Śrīnivāsa Malla. The couple is also known to have built the Kṛṣṇa temple (LAL1450) in the Ko Bāhāḥ Ṭola in 1680 CE (NS 800)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/65867863-c05d-4590-bfbc-4ea0bcfd1339> ;
    rdfs:comment "Amara Siṃha Kumhara and his wife Kīrttimaṅgalā constructed the water spout (hiti) and aphalcā(public shelter) in memory of their deceased son. They also set up a trust supported by land endowments of 2.5ropanīs in Pāko and 5ropanīs in Aphala for their upkeep. The income from these land parcels was used to clear grass from thehitiandphalcāon the 1st solar day of Vaiśākha and to distributesamaybaji, a Newar food set including beaten rice, puffed rice, roasted meat balls, peas, soybeans, etc. Kīrttimaṅgalā was the second daughter of King Śrīnivāsa Malla. The couple is also known to have built the Kṛṣṇa temple (LAL1450) in the Ko Bāhāḥ Ṭola in 1680 CE (NS 800)."@en ;
