<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/6c3da8cc-3c2c-4471-953a-41831d589512_0> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Construction of \"waterholes\""@en ;
    dcterms:description "This inscription, originally set in the wall of the nearby Goḍā Gaṇeśa temple, has been moved to this circular site. Dated 1949 CE (NS 1069), it records that Gaurīlakṣmī Pradhāna, a daughter-in-law from Kathmandu’s Thamel and originally from Pharping, built two \"waterholes\"—one large and one small—at this location. Having grown up locally, she understood the area’s water scarcity and constructed these \"waterholes,\" while also donating land to a religious trust. The \"waterholes\" mentioned in the inscription likely refer to the two ponds noted. Elderly locals recall water consistently seeping from the banks, naturally filling the ponds. The term \"waterholes\" in the inscription seems to reflect this natural replenishment, akin to how waterholes function."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/6c3da8cc-3c2c-4471-953a-41831d589512> ;
    rdfs:comment "This inscription, originally set in the wall of the nearby Goḍā Gaṇeśa temple, has been moved to this circular site. Dated 1949 CE (NS 1069), it records that Gaurīlakṣmī Pradhāna, a daughter-in-law from Kathmandu’s Thamel and originally from Pharping, built two \"waterholes\"—one large and one small—at this location. Having grown up locally, she understood the area’s water scarcity and constructed these \"waterholes,\" while also donating land to a religious trust. The \"waterholes\" mentioned in the inscription likely refer to the two ponds noted. Elderly locals recall water consistently seeping from the banks, naturally filling the ponds. The term \"waterholes\" in the inscription seems to reflect this natural replenishment, akin to how waterholes function."@en ;
