<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/70aa2d18-9dca-473e-9e59-2f426771376a_0> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Pātravaṃśa Hariśaṃkara, along with his mother Rukmanilakṣmī and wife Devīśvarīlakṣmī, from the Tavajhyāra Ṭola, constructed the temple. The inscription, embedded in the northern wall of the temple, further reveals that the donors invited King Yoganarendra Malla of Patan to inspect the temple and other nearby structures. It also documents the installation of statues of other deities, including Bhairava and Halāhala Lokeśvara, as well as the construction of two stone platforms providing shade in the vicinity. The inscription acknowledges the contributions of astrologer Lakṣmīnārāyaṇa, and other individuals such as Guruvācārya Govinda and Sūryarāma, in the successful completion of these constructions."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/70aa2d18-9dca-473e-9e59-2f426771376a> ;
    rdfs:comment "Pātravaṃśa Hariśaṃkara, along with his mother Rukmanilakṣmī and wife Devīśvarīlakṣmī, from the Tavajhyāra Ṭola, constructed the temple. The inscription, embedded in the northern wall of the temple, further reveals that the donors invited King Yoganarendra Malla of Patan to inspect the temple and other nearby structures. It also documents the installation of statues of other deities, including Bhairava and Halāhala Lokeśvara, as well as the construction of two stone platforms providing shade in the vicinity. The inscription acknowledges the contributions of astrologer Lakṣmīnārāyaṇa, and other individuals such as Guruvācārya Govinda and Sūryarāma, in the successful completion of these constructions."@en ;
