<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/74763c55-e9d7-447b-9520-14b2adf86c2b_2> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Temple Restoration and Reconstruction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "In the early 2000s, plans were initiated to restore the temple to its pre-1934 CE form. The collaborative efforts of the Department of Archaeology and the Kathmandu Valley Preservation Trust led to the temple's reconstruction in its original three-tiered design. During this process, plain struts were integrated, and wooden components such as columns and windows were meticulously carved. Despite initial intentions to relocate the Mahāviṣṇu statue from Nāsaḥ Cuka to its original sanctum, a decision was made to establish a new statue at the Mahāviṣṇu Mandira instead."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/74763c55-e9d7-447b-9520-14b2adf86c2b> ;
    rdfs:comment "In the early 2000s, plans were initiated to restore the temple to its pre-1934 CE form. The collaborative efforts of the Department of Archaeology and the Kathmandu Valley Preservation Trust led to the temple's reconstruction in its original three-tiered design. During this process, plain struts were integrated, and wooden components such as columns and windows were meticulously carved. Despite initial intentions to relocate the Mahāviṣṇu statue from Nāsaḥ Cuka to its original sanctum, a decision was made to establish a new statue at the Mahāviṣṇu Mandira instead."@en ;
