<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/77b88f79-3bc0-4373-8673-e60310fa5c97_0> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Reference to a monastic institution"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The inscription, though not connected to the Phalcā, has been temporarily placed in front of its northern plinth. The Licchavi inscription dated 616 CE (Mānadeva Era 40) is significant for the study of Sankhu’s history.D. Vajrācārya(VS 2030, p. 508) has published the left side of the broken inscription. The second half of the Inscription was only recovered in 2014 CE, when Gāḥ Hiti (SKH0142), located behind this Phalca, was renovated. The Inscription contains references to a Tṛpura Vihāra and monastic association of the monks of the Mahāsāṅghika (lit. Great Congregation), a Buddhist school of thought."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/77b88f79-3bc0-4373-8673-e60310fa5c97> ;
    rdfs:comment "The inscription, though not connected to the Phalcā, has been temporarily placed in front of its northern plinth. The Licchavi inscription dated 616 CE (Mānadeva Era 40) is significant for the study of Sankhu’s history.D. Vajrācārya(VS 2030, p. 508) has published the left side of the broken inscription. The second half of the Inscription was only recovered in 2014 CE, when Gāḥ Hiti (SKH0142), located behind this Phalca, was renovated. The Inscription contains references to a Tṛpura Vihāra and monastic association of the monks of the Mahāsāṅghika (lit. Great Congregation), a Buddhist school of thought."@en ;
