<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/7f65c791-011e-43e9-8249-60d1c8614c2f_13> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Erection of a pillar (?)"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The content of the three-line inscription does not record an event but only states the year and the name of the reigning king. It is so far the earliest inscription in a vernacular language (Newari) of Nepal. The year is given in words that literally mean ‘an excess of 3 [and] 9 in the era 200’ (Tamoṭa NS 1097, p. 34). When this is read in reverse, it becomes 200-9-3, i.e., 293. Any year given in non-numeral form or numeral words is read in reverse. The inscription only states ‘during the reign of King Rudradeva in the year 293’ (Tamoṭa NS 1097, p. 34). Therefore, the event is unclear. As the inscription is on the pillar, it probably is the record of its erection."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/7f65c791-011e-43e9-8249-60d1c8614c2f> ;
    rdfs:comment "The content of the three-line inscription does not record an event but only states the year and the name of the reigning king. It is so far the earliest inscription in a vernacular language (Newari) of Nepal. The year is given in words that literally mean ‘an excess of 3 [and] 9 in the era 200’ (Tamoṭa NS 1097, p. 34). When this is read in reverse, it becomes 200-9-3, i.e., 293. Any year given in non-numeral form or numeral words is read in reverse. The inscription only states ‘during the reign of King Rudradeva in the year 293’ (Tamoṭa NS 1097, p. 34). Therefore, the event is unclear. As the inscription is on the pillar, it probably is the record of its erection."@en ;
