<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/834f87e7-78a7-4012-a388-cfe5bb6f51c7_8> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:HistoricalEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Offering a golden statue or crown to Indrāyaṇī"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The inscription records that the sons of Mahaṃtarāma—Bhavānīnārāyaṇa, Śivasundara, and Jogamāna—living in Tãlāchi Ṭola, Ṣābahāla, offered a golden mukaṇa. It is not clear whether the term 'mukaṇa' refers to a statue or a crown. The inscription also directs their descendants to offer a golden roof to the temple if they can, and if they cannot, they should not hinder anyone who wants to offer one. A part of the inscription also mentions that this family originally came from Bhaktapur, and one of their ancestors served as a minister in the royal court of King Jagata Prakāśa Malla. They have also inscribed their genealogy on it."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/834f87e7-78a7-4012-a388-cfe5bb6f51c7> ;
    rdfs:comment "The inscription records that the sons of Mahaṃtarāma—Bhavānīnārāyaṇa, Śivasundara, and Jogamāna—living in Tãlāchi Ṭola, Ṣābahāla, offered a golden mukaṇa. It is not clear whether the term 'mukaṇa' refers to a statue or a crown. The inscription also directs their descendants to offer a golden roof to the temple if they can, and if they cannot, they should not hinder anyone who wants to offer one. A part of the inscription also mentions that this family originally came from Bhaktapur, and one of their ancestors served as a minister in the royal court of King Jagata Prakāśa Malla. They have also inscribed their genealogy on it."@en ;
