<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/85bddcbe-1dbd-43e6-9ff8-58053a32d834_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Consecration ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "While the exact construction date of themaṭharemains undocumented, a surviving stone inscription from 1748 CE (NS 868) records that Rāma Girī formally donated his residential property, including a house, garden, and land in Kvāthandau Ṭola to Vāṇeśvara Mahādeva consecrated within themaṭha. Notably, it mentions the installation of a copper plate inscription documenting this endowment during the reign of Raṇajita Malla - though this inscription appears to remain unpublished in scholarly literature. Academic consensus, based primarily on this epigraphic evidence, maintains that Rāma Girī founded the monastery around 1748 CE (NS 868). The inscription's detailed account of property transfer and deity installation strongly supports this interpretation as historically accurate. The inscription further stipulates specific succession rules: “As long as Rāma Girī's direct descendants exist, no external claims to themaṭha(particularly regarding themahantaposition) shall be recognized; should his lineage cease, authority would transfer to members of Rāma Girī's own sect; in the absence of qualified individuals from his original sect, responsibility would pass to any member of the broader Daśanāmī tradition; and failing all these possibilities, the inscription designates the king as the ultimate guardian of themaṭha.”"@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/85bddcbe-1dbd-43e6-9ff8-58053a32d834> ;
    rdfs:comment "While the exact construction date of themaṭharemains undocumented, a surviving stone inscription from 1748 CE (NS 868) records that Rāma Girī formally donated his residential property, including a house, garden, and land in Kvāthandau Ṭola to Vāṇeśvara Mahādeva consecrated within themaṭha. Notably, it mentions the installation of a copper plate inscription documenting this endowment during the reign of Raṇajita Malla - though this inscription appears to remain unpublished in scholarly literature. Academic consensus, based primarily on this epigraphic evidence, maintains that Rāma Girī founded the monastery around 1748 CE (NS 868). The inscription's detailed account of property transfer and deity installation strongly supports this interpretation as historically accurate. The inscription further stipulates specific succession rules: “As long as Rāma Girī's direct descendants exist, no external claims to themaṭha(particularly regarding themahantaposition) shall be recognized; should his lineage cease, authority would transfer to members of Rāma Girī's own sect; in the absence of qualified individuals from his original sect, responsibility would pass to any member of the broader Daśanāmī tradition; and failing all these possibilities, the inscription designates the king as the ultimate guardian of themaṭha.”"@en ;
