<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/86c994be-020a-11ea-a389-0242ac130002_1> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Reconstruction in 2018-19"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Kvaylāchi Phalcā was reconstructed with the support of the Mohr Foundation (Hamburg, Germany) and local donations. The supervision was conducted by heritage architect Padma Sundar Maharjan from Sunāguthi. Many meetings were held with different community stakeholders, politicians, representatives of the Department of Archaeology and the like, because the Kvaylāchi Phalcā is located at a very particular site. This site has long been under contestation due to road widening plans that would demands its - and many other heritage buildings' - demolition. The reconstruction process took substantially longer due to the long process of negotiations. Dozens of meetings were scheduled, ranging from general discussions about the relationship between road widening and heritage reconstruction, the reconstruction procedure as such, and schedules for financial arrangements. The local youth were also much involved in discussing the work plan, as were the ward presidents. Women, too, were involved in the reconstruction process."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/86c994be-020a-11ea-a389-0242ac130002> ;
    rdfs:comment "Kvaylāchi Phalcā was reconstructed with the support of the Mohr Foundation (Hamburg, Germany) and local donations. The supervision was conducted by heritage architect Padma Sundar Maharjan from Sunāguthi. Many meetings were held with different community stakeholders, politicians, representatives of the Department of Archaeology and the like, because the Kvaylāchi Phalcā is located at a very particular site. This site has long been under contestation due to road widening plans that would demands its - and many other heritage buildings' - demolition. The reconstruction process took substantially longer due to the long process of negotiations. Dozens of meetings were scheduled, ranging from general discussions about the relationship between road widening and heritage reconstruction, the reconstruction procedure as such, and schedules for financial arrangements. The local youth were also much involved in discussing the work plan, as were the ward presidents. Women, too, were involved in the reconstruction process."@en ;
