<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/98358087-f88c-480f-995e-f2d25eb4527b_1> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Destruction in earthquake"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The Temple was destroyed in the 1833 CE earthquake. Under the debris, twenty-two people were killed and thirty others were injured."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/98358087-f88c-480f-995e-f2d25eb4527b> ;
    rdfs:comment "The Temple was destroyed in the 1833 CE earthquake. Under the debris, twenty-two people were killed and thirty others were injured."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
    hg:eventIndex 1 ;
    hg:extractionMethod "source_table" ;
    hg:isEventOf <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/98358087-f88c-480f-995e-f2d25eb4527b> .

<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/98358087-f88c-480f-995e-f2d25eb4527b_2> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Rebuilding"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The original Temple structure did not survive even three decades, collapsing in the 1833 CE earthquakes. The Temple was most likely rebuilt, with the earlier multi-tiered temple being replaced by a much simpler copy of  Bhīmabhakteśvara temple. Gutschow (2011, p.874) writes: \"… Jagannātha temple collapsed in the earthquake of 1833, killing 22 persons. It must have been at this time that an earlier multi-tiered temple was replaced by a hybrid mixture of an outer ambulatory around a core which rises to support a bulbous dome. The finishing of the plaster and the details of the ambulatory’s pillars are devoid of details, as if restricted resources resulted in a much simplified copy of the Bhīmabhakteśvara temple. Its annual proportion support the assertion that it is a replacement: From the centre of a comparatively low ground floor without pilasters but with an ambulatory, a pilastered first floor complete with bottom mouldings rises up as if this was to be the actual temple”."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/98358087-f88c-480f-995e-f2d25eb4527b> ;
    rdfs:comment "The original Temple structure did not survive even three decades, collapsing in the 1833 CE earthquakes. The Temple was most likely rebuilt, with the earlier multi-tiered temple being replaced by a much simpler copy of  Bhīmabhakteśvara temple. Gutschow (2011, p.874) writes: \"… Jagannātha temple collapsed in the earthquake of 1833, killing 22 persons. It must have been at this time that an earlier multi-tiered temple was replaced by a hybrid mixture of an outer ambulatory around a core which rises to support a bulbous dome. The finishing of the plaster and the details of the ambulatory’s pillars are devoid of details, as if restricted resources resulted in a much simplified copy of the Bhīmabhakteśvara temple. Its annual proportion support the assertion that it is a replacement: From the centre of a comparatively low ground floor without pilasters but with an ambulatory, a pilastered first floor complete with bottom mouldings rises up as if this was to be the actual temple”."@en ;
