<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/a2cfeca7-18cd-4ba6-8793-4390725a1375_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "This inscription records Hṛdayaratna Gvaṃgaḥ and his wife, Manitha[kuṃ], establishing Hṛdayeśvara Mahādeva in the temple. While the inscription has been somewhat damaged, obscuring the year of its construction, Pruscha (1975, p. 230) reports the existence of an inscription from 1865 CE, which corresponds to VS 1922. The temple's architecture and the artifacts found here seem to originate around the same period, suggesting that Pruscha likely read the date from this inscription. Hṛdayaratna additionally established various other deities, including Hṛdayavināyaka, Hṛdayeśvarī, Hṛdayanārāyaṇa, Garuḍeśvara, Trailokeśvara, Umāmaheśvara, Viśveśvara, Gaṇeśa, Hanumān, Kāmadeva, Durlabheśvara, Umeśvara, Maṇikeśvara, Vaṭuka, Joginī, Kṣetrapāla, Brahmāyaṇī, Mohinī, Vaiṣṇavī, Vārāhī, Cāmuṇḍā, and Mahālakṣmī. He also built anāgaṃchẽdirectly in front of the temple, along with the Nārāyaṇa temple accompanied by a Garuḍa pillar. Ample land parcels were endowed for their worship and upkeep."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/a2cfeca7-18cd-4ba6-8793-4390725a1375> ;
    rdfs:comment "This inscription records Hṛdayaratna Gvaṃgaḥ and his wife, Manitha[kuṃ], establishing Hṛdayeśvara Mahādeva in the temple. While the inscription has been somewhat damaged, obscuring the year of its construction, Pruscha (1975, p. 230) reports the existence of an inscription from 1865 CE, which corresponds to VS 1922. The temple's architecture and the artifacts found here seem to originate around the same period, suggesting that Pruscha likely read the date from this inscription. Hṛdayaratna additionally established various other deities, including Hṛdayavināyaka, Hṛdayeśvarī, Hṛdayanārāyaṇa, Garuḍeśvara, Trailokeśvara, Umāmaheśvara, Viśveśvara, Gaṇeśa, Hanumān, Kāmadeva, Durlabheśvara, Umeśvara, Maṇikeśvara, Vaṭuka, Joginī, Kṣetrapāla, Brahmāyaṇī, Mohinī, Vaiṣṇavī, Vārāhī, Cāmuṇḍā, and Mahālakṣmī. He also built anāgaṃchẽdirectly in front of the temple, along with the Nārāyaṇa temple accompanied by a Garuḍa pillar. Ample land parcels were endowed for their worship and upkeep."@en ;
