<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/adc2f4ee-e99a-11e9-b125-0242ac130002_2> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Reconstruction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "In June 2019, the Phalcāwas dismantled and all building materials were discarded. The reconstruction started in July 2019 and incorporated traditional design features. The painting of the snake was preserved and covered with a protective layer of foam. The foundations were rebuilt with reinforced cement concrete beams and the floor was recast as a 4-inch-thick steel reinforced concrete pad. The walls were constructed using modern brick withdāci āpāfacing. Six newly carved hard wood columns and highly decorated capitals and beams replaced the earlier plain timber frames. The first floor received a projecting central window and two flanking windows, all carved in great detail and vaguely resembling a late 19th century style.According to a local informant, the design for the Phalcā was provided by Rohit Ranjitkar from the KVPT. Working drawings were made by Anil Basukala.The project was conceptualised by Śobhā Śākya and funded through the Parliamentary Development Fund (Sāṃsada Vikāsa Koṣa/ सांसद विकास कोष)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/adc2f4ee-e99a-11e9-b125-0242ac130002> ;
    rdfs:comment "In June 2019, the Phalcāwas dismantled and all building materials were discarded. The reconstruction started in July 2019 and incorporated traditional design features. The painting of the snake was preserved and covered with a protective layer of foam. The foundations were rebuilt with reinforced cement concrete beams and the floor was recast as a 4-inch-thick steel reinforced concrete pad. The walls were constructed using modern brick withdāci āpāfacing. Six newly carved hard wood columns and highly decorated capitals and beams replaced the earlier plain timber frames. The first floor received a projecting central window and two flanking windows, all carved in great detail and vaguely resembling a late 19th century style.According to a local informant, the design for the Phalcā was provided by Rohit Ranjitkar from the KVPT. Working drawings were made by Anil Basukala.The project was conceptualised by Śobhā Śākya and funded through the Parliamentary Development Fund (Sāṃsada Vikāsa Koṣa/ सांसद विकास कोष)."@en ;
