<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/afa386ca-8eea-11ea-bd59-0242ac130005_3> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction of the hiti"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Together with his wives Indralakṣmī and Jayalakṣmī, his son Phyakudeva and his daughter Śuṃdaralakṣmī, Tuhūdeva Vajrācārya had this Hiti constructed on Thursday 8thof the bright fortnight of NS 535 (1415 CE). Since it was dry at the time, a Tantric ritual was performed in advance of its construction. He also bestowed nineropanīsof land upon the Hiti to fund its continued maintenance. The copperplate regulates that the stone which was placed after the Tantric ritual should never be uncovered. In addition to this, honey, dirts, soap and leather shoes should not be taken into the Hiti area.The copperplate mentions that there is a stone inscription attached to the Caitya, and that this provides more information about the construction of the Hiti. However, this inscription is missing."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/afa386ca-8eea-11ea-bd59-0242ac130005> ;
    rdfs:comment "Together with his wives Indralakṣmī and Jayalakṣmī, his son Phyakudeva and his daughter Śuṃdaralakṣmī, Tuhūdeva Vajrācārya had this Hiti constructed on Thursday 8thof the bright fortnight of NS 535 (1415 CE). Since it was dry at the time, a Tantric ritual was performed in advance of its construction. He also bestowed nineropanīsof land upon the Hiti to fund its continued maintenance. The copperplate regulates that the stone which was placed after the Tantric ritual should never be uncovered. In addition to this, honey, dirts, soap and leather shoes should not be taken into the Hiti area.The copperplate mentions that there is a stone inscription attached to the Caitya, and that this provides more information about the construction of the Hiti. However, this inscription is missing."@en ;
