<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/b23905f8-e7f2-11e9-8bc4-0242ac130002_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "PhotoKathmandu"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Atāḥ Phalcā was part of the first PhotoKathmandu festival, which included an exhibition and curated walk entitled 'Patis in Patan'. It was curated by Sujan Chitrakar (Kathmandu University, Centre for Art & Design), Rajendra Shakya (South Asia Institute branch office, Heidelberg) and Christiane Brosius (Heidelberg Centre for Transcultural Studies, HCTS) in November 2015 following the 2015 earthquakes. The project focused on the everyday lives of people in public spaces such as the arcaded rest houses. Photographs were taken of elderly women using the Phalcā, who also spoke with interviewees about their lives and the role of the Phalcā in them. A selection of these photographs was exhibited on the walls inside the building and next to it.The Phalcā has been used as an exhibition space to tell the stories of the women who sit in it every day, and to create awareness of and interest in the site as a social space."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/b23905f8-e7f2-11e9-8bc4-0242ac130002> ;
    rdfs:comment "Atāḥ Phalcā was part of the first PhotoKathmandu festival, which included an exhibition and curated walk entitled 'Patis in Patan'. It was curated by Sujan Chitrakar (Kathmandu University, Centre for Art & Design), Rajendra Shakya (South Asia Institute branch office, Heidelberg) and Christiane Brosius (Heidelberg Centre for Transcultural Studies, HCTS) in November 2015 following the 2015 earthquakes. The project focused on the everyday lives of people in public spaces such as the arcaded rest houses. Photographs were taken of elderly women using the Phalcā, who also spoke with interviewees about their lives and the role of the Phalcā in them. A selection of these photographs was exhibited on the walls inside the building and next to it.The Phalcā has been used as an exhibition space to tell the stories of the women who sit in it every day, and to create awareness of and interest in the site as a social space."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
