<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/b7024813-cfb9-4485-b76c-fe67ba7ec8ea_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Construction of Thaṃthu Durbar"@en ;
    dcterms:description "An inscription found within the Hiti complex, though unrelated to it, records the construction of the Thaṇthu Palace by King Jitāmitra Malla on the 10th day of the bright half of Āṣāḍha, 1678 CE (NS 798), during the tenure of Prime Minister Bhāgirāma. Built in honor of the king’s tutelary deity, the inscription decrees its preservation and mandates royal restoration if damaged. It further notes that the administrative rules of the Bhaktapur palace—covering courtyards, gardens, pavilions, andhitis—also applied here, naming Ugra Malla and Bhūpatīndra Malla, and designating the palace as “Thaṇthu Rājakula” with its outer courtyard “Siddhicuka.”"@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/b7024813-cfb9-4485-b76c-fe67ba7ec8ea> ;
    rdfs:comment "An inscription found within the Hiti complex, though unrelated to it, records the construction of the Thaṇthu Palace by King Jitāmitra Malla on the 10th day of the bright half of Āṣāḍha, 1678 CE (NS 798), during the tenure of Prime Minister Bhāgirāma. Built in honor of the king’s tutelary deity, the inscription decrees its preservation and mandates royal restoration if damaged. It further notes that the administrative rules of the Bhaktapur palace—covering courtyards, gardens, pavilions, andhitis—also applied here, naming Ugra Malla and Bhūpatīndra Malla, and designating the palace as “Thaṇthu Rājakula” with its outer courtyard “Siddhicuka.”"@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
