<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/b8b26a0c-02a8-11ea-a389-0242ac130002_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:DestructionEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Renovation of Khapiṁchẽ Phalcā (now lost)"@en ;
    dcterms:description "At the request of members of the local community, Patan Conservation and Development Programme undertook the repair of the Khapiṁchẽ Phalcā in March 1994. Damage had begun from the roof, as is common in such work, and the rafters, plankings and eaves boards were rotten due to several years of monsoons. However, some of the timber planks were reusable and so were kept. The top half of the back wall was in a very poor condition because it had been built with sun dried bricks. The plinth was in a good condition.During the repair work, the roof structure was replaced with new sal timber and planks with a water proof membrane of tarfelt. The timber beam above the columns was also replaced with sal timber. All of the original timber columns were reused. The wainscote on the back wall was provided at the request of local elders. A few of the plinth's bricks were reused and the top half of the back wall was rebuilt using traditional mud mortar and old ma-aapa.The total cost, including construction, implementation and supervision, came to Rs 64,846.00"@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/b8b26a0c-02a8-11ea-a389-0242ac130002> ;
    rdfs:comment "At the request of members of the local community, Patan Conservation and Development Programme undertook the repair of the Khapiṁchẽ Phalcā in March 1994. Damage had begun from the roof, as is common in such work, and the rafters, plankings and eaves boards were rotten due to several years of monsoons. However, some of the timber planks were reusable and so were kept. The top half of the back wall was in a very poor condition because it had been built with sun dried bricks. The plinth was in a good condition.During the repair work, the roof structure was replaced with new sal timber and planks with a water proof membrane of tarfelt. The timber beam above the columns was also replaced with sal timber. All of the original timber columns were reused. The wainscote on the back wall was provided at the request of local elders. A few of the plinth's bricks were reused and the top half of the back wall was rebuilt using traditional mud mortar and old ma-aapa.The total cost, including construction, implementation and supervision, came to Rs 64,846.00"@en ;
