<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/b8f4c2b9-7ba1-4c7d-bc70-40d99b5c4a81_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Pavement of the path"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Rāmakṛṣṇa Kũvara and his younger brother Jayakṛṣṇa Kũvara, both ancestors of Jaṅga Bahādura Rāṇā, are said to have laid stone slabs on the path from the Paśupati temple to Guhyeśvarī. They also set up a trust that funded necessary renovations of the arcaded platforms that were built for brahmins and poor people to live in within the Paśupati area. The building and land that Rāmakṛṣṇa Kũvara received as a reward from Pṛthvīnārāyaṇa were endowed to the trust (G. Ṭaṇḍana VS 2056, p.278-280). The donated building and land originally belonged to Avadhūta Siṃha, an illegitimate son of King Raṇajita Malla of Bhaktapur."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/b8f4c2b9-7ba1-4c7d-bc70-40d99b5c4a81> ;
    rdfs:comment "Rāmakṛṣṇa Kũvara and his younger brother Jayakṛṣṇa Kũvara, both ancestors of Jaṅga Bahādura Rāṇā, are said to have laid stone slabs on the path from the Paśupati temple to Guhyeśvarī. They also set up a trust that funded necessary renovations of the arcaded platforms that were built for brahmins and poor people to live in within the Paśupati area. The building and land that Rāmakṛṣṇa Kũvara received as a reward from Pṛthvīnārāyaṇa were endowed to the trust (G. Ṭaṇḍana VS 2056, p.278-280). The donated building and land originally belonged to Avadhūta Siṃha, an illegitimate son of King Raṇajita Malla of Bhaktapur."@en ;
