<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/b947bd74-dc11-46f3-afd4-189d70f64e3c_0> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Erecting garūḍa pillar"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Anuparama Gupta, a powerful figure during the Licchavi period, erected a Garuḍa column in front of the temple and inscribed verses dedicated to Dvaipāyana Vyāsa. Anuparama Gupta's wife was Ābhirī Gominī, and his son was Bhauma Gupta, who later became a powerful figure in the Licchavi administration. This inscription, with its mature writing style and philosophical content, is considered of high quality. While the inscription lacks a specific date or the name of the reigning king, based on its script, it is attributed to the reign of Vasantadeva, who succeeded Mānadeva. A human-like Garuḍa statues are customarily erected in front of Viṣṇu statues in Nepal. These statues often resemble the face of the devotee sponsoring the statue (G. Vajrācārya VS 2033, p. 127). Possibly, Anuparama Gupta established this statue to reflect his own likeness. As Anuparama Gupta's wife, Ābhirī Gominī, installed the Anuparameśvara Śivaliṅga at Paśupati in 540 CE (ŚS 462) (D. Vajrācārya VS 2030, pp. 170-171), it can be inferred that Anuparama Gupta died around that time. Therefore, the Garuḍa column in front of the Satyanārāyaṇa temple must predate 540 CE (cp. Acharya 2007, pp. 30-32)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/b947bd74-dc11-46f3-afd4-189d70f64e3c> ;
    rdfs:comment "Anuparama Gupta, a powerful figure during the Licchavi period, erected a Garuḍa column in front of the temple and inscribed verses dedicated to Dvaipāyana Vyāsa. Anuparama Gupta's wife was Ābhirī Gominī, and his son was Bhauma Gupta, who later became a powerful figure in the Licchavi administration. This inscription, with its mature writing style and philosophical content, is considered of high quality. While the inscription lacks a specific date or the name of the reigning king, based on its script, it is attributed to the reign of Vasantadeva, who succeeded Mānadeva. A human-like Garuḍa statues are customarily erected in front of Viṣṇu statues in Nepal. These statues often resemble the face of the devotee sponsoring the statue (G. Vajrācārya VS 2033, p. 127). Possibly, Anuparama Gupta established this statue to reflect his own likeness. As Anuparama Gupta's wife, Ābhirī Gominī, installed the Anuparameśvara Śivaliṅga at Paśupati in 540 CE (ŚS 462) (D. Vajrācārya VS 2030, pp. 170-171), it can be inferred that Anuparama Gupta died around that time. Therefore, the Garuḍa column in front of the Satyanārāyaṇa temple must predate 540 CE (cp. Acharya 2007, pp. 30-32)."@en ;
