<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/c0754b90-e124-4d81-a943-62db5de58de1_1> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:HistoricalEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Offering of a bell (object no. NWK0170-007)"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Dharmaśīlavantīdevī (text: Dharmasīlavaṃtidevī), the senior queen of King Pṛthvī Vīra Vikrama Śāha, offered the bell in the name of Lord Kṛṣṇa. The name of Pṛthvī Vīra Vikrama's senior queen was Ratnadivyeśvarī, who hailed from Punjab, India. She married King Pṛthvī Vīra Vikrama Śāha in 1887 CE (VS 1943) (see P. Rāja VS 2071, p. 13). On the bell installed after the king's death, her name was inscribed as Dharmaśīlavantīdevī, not Ratnadivyeśvarī, raising uncertainty about why the name was changed. As the bell is offered to Kṛṣṇa, it could have been brought from somewhere else and installed in this temple."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/c0754b90-e124-4d81-a943-62db5de58de1> ;
    rdfs:comment "Dharmaśīlavantīdevī (text: Dharmasīlavaṃtidevī), the senior queen of King Pṛthvī Vīra Vikrama Śāha, offered the bell in the name of Lord Kṛṣṇa. The name of Pṛthvī Vīra Vikrama's senior queen was Ratnadivyeśvarī, who hailed from Punjab, India. She married King Pṛthvī Vīra Vikrama Śāha in 1887 CE (VS 1943) (see P. Rāja VS 2071, p. 13). On the bell installed after the king's death, her name was inscribed as Dharmaśīlavantīdevī, not Ratnadivyeśvarī, raising uncertainty about why the name was changed. As the bell is offered to Kṛṣṇa, it could have been brought from somewhere else and installed in this temple."@en ;
