<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/ca7dd19e-f7af-11e9-8649-0242ac130002_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Graffiti on front facade"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The facade of the Sataḥ was spray-painted by Seb Toussaint, an Anglo-French artist. The monument caught the attention of artists and cultural actors and caused an agitated public debate surrounding the definition of ownership of endangered cultural heritage. Seb Toussaint and his Outsiders Krew were accused of abusing religious sites and local people for the ‘wrong’ purposes; i.e. harming sites through sacrilege and 'insulting' their 'authentic' purposes instead of working to beautify underprivileged sites. The art group travels around the world and visits impoverished and neglected urban settlements, where they invite local inhabitants to ‘participate’ in the beautification of their area by selecting meaningful words that the artists then, sometimes with the help of local artists or laymen, paint on the walls of residential houses. The Sataḥ at Purohitaghāṭa was the first example of a religious compound being used in the art project, which is why it enraged some groups even a few weeks after the wall had been already painted and sprayed. The question of ‘Whose heritage’ was raised, and ranged between accusations of iconoclasm against a Hindu temple (though the Shiva temple lied behind the Sataḥ at Purohitaghāṭa) to damaging heritage structures (such as spraying unremovable paint onto a rare window design from 1883). One critic argued that, “the Shiva Temple, though not on the World Heritage Site List, has something to contribute to the remarkable cultural heritage and legacy of Nepal. Any kind of intruding and defacing it would be overlooking history of the place and hurting sentiments of my people.” (inThapa 2014)The case is also interesting because it draws attention to the fact that the Satah had long been in a dilapidated state and sidelined by public attention. Some Nepalis questioned why this had been the case for so long, and why it took foreign artwork to notice this. As such, these voices found the accusations ambivalent, claiming that had the site been recognised as heritage, people would have cared about its neglected state beforehand. The fact that it was left uncared for for so long was seen as a sign of ignorance on behalf of the Nepalese people vis-à-vis their own heritage.Thapa (2014)refers to Niels Gutschow’s mention of the site in his third volume of Architecture of Newars: “The sattal at Purohitghat was one of the smaller endowments, but probably the most fascinating one. The baluster columns and the cusped arches of the arcade follow early 19th century prototypes, but the first floor windows went beyond the scope of carved window frames … The frames of the side openings add to the stunning design, contributing to a move that culminates in the flower motifs that crown the central opening. In a playful manner, curled appendages frame the entire window, flush with the wall“ (see primary image).Mahaju (2015)connects cultural heritage to the idea of national progress and development for which it has often been sacrificed, showing that cultural heritage has been dramatically endangered in times of urban transformation: “in 1993/4, during the construction of the Bagmati Bridge in Thapathali, the government tore down a historic temple and a pati. What remains of the complex is cramped away in one corner. The government had assured the public that those structures will be relocated with due honour as it merits a place of worship. Twenty one years hence, the promise still hangs in the air. The recent road widening also has upset conservationists and heritage practitioners, common people notwithstanding, at how the government has treated places of utmost importance.”"@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/ca7dd19e-f7af-11e9-8649-0242ac130002> ;
    rdfs:comment "The facade of the Sataḥ was spray-painted by Seb Toussaint, an Anglo-French artist. The monument caught the attention of artists and cultural actors and caused an agitated public debate surrounding the definition of ownership of endangered cultural heritage. Seb Toussaint and his Outsiders Krew were accused of abusing religious sites and local people for the ‘wrong’ purposes; i.e. harming sites through sacrilege and 'insulting' their 'authentic' purposes instead of working to beautify underprivileged sites. The art group travels around the world and visits impoverished and neglected urban settlements, where they invite local inhabitants to ‘participate’ in the beautification of their area by selecting meaningful words that the artists then, sometimes with the help of local artists or laymen, paint on the walls of residential houses. The Sataḥ at Purohitaghāṭa was the first example of a religious compound being used in the art project, which is why it enraged some groups even a few weeks after the wall had been already painted and sprayed. The question of ‘Whose heritage’ was raised, and ranged between accusations of iconoclasm against a Hindu temple (though the Shiva temple lied behind the Sataḥ at Purohitaghāṭa) to damaging heritage structures (such as spraying unremovable paint onto a rare window design from 1883). One critic argued that, “the Shiva Temple, though not on the World Heritage Site List, has something to contribute to the remarkable cultural heritage and legacy of Nepal. Any kind of intruding and defacing it would be overlooking history of the place and hurting sentiments of my people.” (inThapa 2014)The case is also interesting because it draws attention to the fact that the Satah had long been in a dilapidated state and sidelined by public attention. Some Nepalis questioned why this had been the case for so long, and why it took foreign artwork to notice this. As such, these voices found the accusations ambivalent, claiming that had the site been recognised as heritage, people would have cared about its neglected state beforehand. The fact that it was left uncared for for so long was seen as a sign of ignorance on behalf of the Nepalese people vis-à-vis their own heritage.Thapa (2014)refers to Niels Gutschow’s mention of the site in his third volume of Architecture of Newars: “The sattal at Purohitghat was one of the smaller endowments, but probably the most fascinating one. The baluster columns and the cusped arches of the arcade follow early 19th century prototypes, but the first floor windows went beyond the scope of carved window frames … The frames of the side openings add to the stunning design, contributing to a move that culminates in the flower motifs that crown the central opening. In a playful manner, curled appendages frame the entire window, flush with the wall“ (see primary image).Mahaju (2015)connects cultural heritage to the idea of national progress and development for which it has often been sacrificed, showing that cultural heritage has been dramatically endangered in times of urban transformation: “in 1993/4, during the construction of the Bagmati Bridge in Thapathali, the government tore down a historic temple and a pati. What remains of the complex is cramped away in one corner. The government had assured the public that those structures will be relocated with due honour as it merits a place of worship. Twenty one years hence, the promise still hangs in the air. The recent road widening also has upset conservationists and heritage practitioners, common people notwithstanding, at how the government has treated places of utmost importance.”"@en ;
