<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/cdd89090-bf21-4777-b008-2f5ce5397940_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:RitualEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "An inscription on the upper part of the well tells us about its builders. It says that the well was built in 1928 CE (VS 1985) during the reign of King Tribhuvan and Candra Śamśera. The builders were Viṣṇulāl Mākaḥju, his wife Vālamāyā, and his brother Kṛṣṇalāl. Viṣṇulāl was the grandson of Cakanārāye (Cakranārāyaṇa) and the son of Mahādyau (Mahādeva), and he was a wealthy resident living near Ganeśasthān in the Oṭola area of Dhulikhel (local resident: Mākaḥju, male, age 61). The inscription also expresses the family’s wishes for their sons’ and grandsons’ success, prosperity for future generations, and for their family name to continue. For many years, the family followed the tradition of cleaning the well every year onSithi Nakha, a festival for cleaning water sources. This practice stopped around the 1990s (VS 2050), but the well still flows and continues to serve the community."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/cdd89090-bf21-4777-b008-2f5ce5397940> ;
    rdfs:comment "An inscription on the upper part of the well tells us about its builders. It says that the well was built in 1928 CE (VS 1985) during the reign of King Tribhuvan and Candra Śamśera. The builders were Viṣṇulāl Mākaḥju, his wife Vālamāyā, and his brother Kṛṣṇalāl. Viṣṇulāl was the grandson of Cakanārāye (Cakranārāyaṇa) and the son of Mahādyau (Mahādeva), and he was a wealthy resident living near Ganeśasthān in the Oṭola area of Dhulikhel (local resident: Mākaḥju, male, age 61). The inscription also expresses the family’s wishes for their sons’ and grandsons’ success, prosperity for future generations, and for their family name to continue. For many years, the family followed the tradition of cleaning the well every year onSithi Nakha, a festival for cleaning water sources. This practice stopped around the 1990s (VS 2050), but the well still flows and continues to serve the community."@en ;
