<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/df484d8d-869e-4447-a7a9-a38a889ed310_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Construction of a maṇdapa (phalcā) and establishment of a śivaliṅga"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The oldest inscription in the complex, dated to 1669 CE (NS 789), is affixed to the southwest corner of the site but is unrelated to the present Tileśvara temple. Installed during the reign of King Śrīnivāsa Malla, it records that descendants of a Brahmin named Śakatāja—who had accompanied King Jayahariśivadeva—built a maṇḍapa and installed a deity at this site. It includes a brief genealogy and mentions land endowments (guṭhī) for temple rituals. However, neither the Śivaharīśvara-liṅga nor the endowed lands survive today."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/df484d8d-869e-4447-a7a9-a38a889ed310> ;
    rdfs:comment "The oldest inscription in the complex, dated to 1669 CE (NS 789), is affixed to the southwest corner of the site but is unrelated to the present Tileśvara temple. Installed during the reign of King Śrīnivāsa Malla, it records that descendants of a Brahmin named Śakatāja—who had accompanied King Jayahariśivadeva—built a maṇḍapa and installed a deity at this site. It includes a brief genealogy and mentions land endowments (guṭhī) for temple rituals. However, neither the Śivaharīśvara-liṅga nor the endowed lands survive today."@en ;
