<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/e2a4580b-0699-44bf-b0b3-561da8d9fa93_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:HistoricalEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Licchavi inscription 1"@en ;
    dcterms:description "This inscription is not related to thehiti. Although the date of this inscription has already eroded, it is clear that it was installed during the joint rule of King Bhīmārjunadeva and Jiṣṇugupta. It declares the reduction of levies onmalla(a kind of pet animal) and their babies to facilitate the residents of Dakṣiṇakolīgrāma (D.Vajrācārya, VS 2030, p. 425)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/e2a4580b-0699-44bf-b0b3-561da8d9fa93> ;
    rdfs:comment "This inscription is not related to thehiti. Although the date of this inscription has already eroded, it is clear that it was installed during the joint rule of King Bhīmārjunadeva and Jiṣṇugupta. It declares the reduction of levies onmalla(a kind of pet animal) and their babies to facilitate the residents of Dakṣiṇakolīgrāma (D.Vajrācārya, VS 2030, p. 425)."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
    hg:eventIndex 0 ;
    hg:extractionMethod "source_table" ;
    hg:isEventOf <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/e2a4580b-0699-44bf-b0b3-561da8d9fa93> .

<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/e2a4580b-0699-44bf-b0b3-561da8d9fa93_1> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:TransferOfCustody ;
    rdfs:label "Licchavi inscription 2"@en ;
    dcterms:description "This inscription is unrelated to thehiti.It was installed during the joint rule of King Bhīmārjunadeva and Viṣṇugupta, dated Mānadeva Samvat 64 (640 CE). It documents the rulers' pleasur with the efforts of people for having pulled a large stone for carving a statue of Viṣṇu. Viṣṇugupta granted the residents of Dakṣiṇakolīgrāma the privilege to wear jewelry, with the exception of anklets and armlets of specific kinds. The inscription also highlights Viṣṇugupta's commissioning of a large reclining statue of Viṣṇu in a pond at the base of the northern hills (Gopālarājavaṃśāvalīfol. 22). The inscription confirms what is recorded in the chronicle. Moreover, the inscription reveals a shift in Viṣṇugupta's approach toward justice. He showed leniency towards the residents of Dakṣiṇakolīgrāma concerning theft, murder, and crimes against the state. The decree in the inscription specifies that only the individual convicted of these crimes would face punishment, excluding their family members. This contrasts with the prevailing practice at the time, where even the family members of convicts were punished as accessories to the crime (D. Vajrācārya, VS 2030, p. 447)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/e2a4580b-0699-44bf-b0b3-561da8d9fa93> ;
    rdfs:comment "This inscription is unrelated to thehiti.It was installed during the joint rule of King Bhīmārjunadeva and Viṣṇugupta, dated Mānadeva Samvat 64 (640 CE). It documents the rulers' pleasur with the efforts of people for having pulled a large stone for carving a statue of Viṣṇu. Viṣṇugupta granted the residents of Dakṣiṇakolīgrāma the privilege to wear jewelry, with the exception of anklets and armlets of specific kinds. The inscription also highlights Viṣṇugupta's commissioning of a large reclining statue of Viṣṇu in a pond at the base of the northern hills (Gopālarājavaṃśāvalīfol. 22). The inscription confirms what is recorded in the chronicle. Moreover, the inscription reveals a shift in Viṣṇugupta's approach toward justice. He showed leniency towards the residents of Dakṣiṇakolīgrāma concerning theft, murder, and crimes against the state. The decree in the inscription specifies that only the individual convicted of these crimes would face punishment, excluding their family members. This contrasts with the prevailing practice at the time, where even the family members of convicts were punished as accessories to the crime (D. Vajrācārya, VS 2030, p. 447)."@en ;
