<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/ec4c5daf-9b79-4bb0-8a72-00410822e759_0> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Enshrinement ;
    rdfs:label "Initial installation of the statues"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The inscription states that Bhavānīśaṅkara installed images of Akṣobhya, Śāriputra, and Maudgalyāyana in his house of his own volition. It does not clarify whether these images were installed within the conceptual framework of abāhāḥ. Thus, the inscription reveals only that a Buddhist triad was originally established devotionally and at his own initiative within a private residence. Before installing the images of Akṣobhya, Śāriputra, and Maudgalyāyana, Bhavānīśaṅkara had installed his own portrait image (KAT1850-010), along with those of other family members, in Lāykū Bahī on nineth of bright fortnight of Kārtika of the same year. This fact is confirmed by the inscription carved on the pedestal of that portrait (KAT1850_I_001). Interestingly, the inscription found at the present site also records the details of this portrait installation. In it, Bhavānīśaṅkara allocates plots of land as sources of income and stipulates various ritual provisions for the annual observances related both to the images of Buddha, Śāriputra, and Maudgalyāyana, and to his own portrait installed in Lāykū Bahī."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/ec4c5daf-9b79-4bb0-8a72-00410822e759> ;
    rdfs:comment "The inscription states that Bhavānīśaṅkara installed images of Akṣobhya, Śāriputra, and Maudgalyāyana in his house of his own volition. It does not clarify whether these images were installed within the conceptual framework of abāhāḥ. Thus, the inscription reveals only that a Buddhist triad was originally established devotionally and at his own initiative within a private residence. Before installing the images of Akṣobhya, Śāriputra, and Maudgalyāyana, Bhavānīśaṅkara had installed his own portrait image (KAT1850-010), along with those of other family members, in Lāykū Bahī on nineth of bright fortnight of Kārtika of the same year. This fact is confirmed by the inscription carved on the pedestal of that portrait (KAT1850_I_001). Interestingly, the inscription found at the present site also records the details of this portrait installation. In it, Bhavānīśaṅkara allocates plots of land as sources of income and stipulates various ritual provisions for the annual observances related both to the images of Buddha, Śāriputra, and Maudgalyāyana, and to his own portrait installed in Lāykū Bahī."@en ;
