        hg:Enshrinement ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction of the monument"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The origins of the temple's establishment have been marked by ambiguity and scholarly debate. According to theDevamālāvaṃśāvalī, a subsequent historical account, the temple's creation is attributed to the reign of King Pratāpa Malla, who is said to have erected the temple and introduced the enshrinement of the Jagannātha triad. However, this assertion has been challenged by the presence of an inscription dating back to 1563 CE (NS 683) on the Caturvyūha statue within the temple premises. This inscription provides evidence that the temple predates the era of King Pratāpa Malla, whose rule spanned from 1641 to 1674 CE."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325> ;
    rdfs:comment "The origins of the temple's establishment have been marked by ambiguity and scholarly debate. According to theDevamālāvaṃśāvalī, a subsequent historical account, the temple's creation is attributed to the reign of King Pratāpa Malla, who is said to have erected the temple and introduced the enshrinement of the Jagannātha triad. However, this assertion has been challenged by the presence of an inscription dating back to 1563 CE (NS 683) on the Caturvyūha statue within the temple premises. This inscription provides evidence that the temple predates the era of King Pratāpa Malla, whose rule spanned from 1641 to 1674 CE."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
    hg:eventIndex 0 ;
    hg:extractionMethod "source_table" ;
    hg:isEventOf <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325> .

<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325_1> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:HistoricalEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Royal order"@en ;
    dcterms:description "A copperplate inscription can be seen adorning the upper part of the temple's eastern entrance. This inscription imparts a directive to thedaśanāmīascetics. The inscription emphasizes the importance of harmony and unity among the ascetics, stipulating that disputes and conflicts among them are to be unequivocally avoided. The royal command further calls for a collective commitment to peaceful coexistence and the adherence to appropriate conduct. This inscription serves as a testament to the socio-religious governance of the time, highlighting the influence of the royal authority in fostering a cohesive and disciplined spiritual community within the temple's precincts."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325> ;
    rdfs:comment "A copperplate inscription can be seen adorning the upper part of the temple's eastern entrance. This inscription imparts a directive to thedaśanāmīascetics. The inscription emphasizes the importance of harmony and unity among the ascetics, stipulating that disputes and conflicts among them are to be unequivocally avoided. The royal command further calls for a collective commitment to peaceful coexistence and the adherence to appropriate conduct. This inscription serves as a testament to the socio-religious governance of the time, highlighting the influence of the royal authority in fostering a cohesive and disciplined spiritual community within the temple's precincts."@en ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/agent/antigravity_agent> ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/activity/heritagegraph_kg_construction> ;
    hg:eventIndex 1 ;
    hg:extractionMethod "source_table" ;
    hg:isEventOf <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325> .

<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325_2> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Poems by King Pratāpa Malla"@en ;
    dcterms:description "In the history of the temple, an event involves inscriptions found on large stone tanks along its eastern side. These inscriptions, dating back to 1654 CE (NS 774), were written by King Pratāpa Malla. In these writings, the king expresses his deep respect for various goddesses through his poetry. King Pratāpa Malla's words carry an important message: he believes that being a true scholar goes beyond reading words. He suggests that understanding and connecting with the meaning of the verses is what truly matters. He even mentions that those who can not grasp this meaning lead less meaningful lives."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/eeb8d34e-c3f4-453e-bfac-331cefee0325> ;
    rdfs:comment "In the history of the temple, an event involves inscriptions found on large stone tanks along its eastern side. These inscriptions, dating back to 1654 CE (NS 774), were written by King Pratāpa Malla. In these writings, the king expresses his deep respect for various goddesses through his poetry. King Pratāpa Malla's words carry an important message: he believes that being a true scholar goes beyond reading words. He suggests that understanding and connecting with the meaning of the verses is what truly matters. He even mentions that those who can not grasp this meaning lead less meaningful lives."@en ;
