<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/f6f28540-6aa7-4a61-abd1-03d8250aa25f_0> a crm:E12_Production,
        crm:E5_Event,
        hg:Production ;
    rdfs:label "Initial construction"@en ;
    dcterms:description "King Amṛta Malla had commissioned the construction of asataḥat the Sālkhā Ṭola and donated many plots of land to feed ascetics from foreign lands at the Sataḥ (N. YogīVS 2012, p. 61-62). The King got the Sataḥ made in the name of his deceased wife Lakṣmīdevī and two of his late sons Abhayasiṃha and Kṛṣṇasiṃha, along with his daughters-in-law Jayatudevī, Phikudevī, and Herādevī. A line of an inscription installed at the Sataḥ refers to Śaṅkarapaṭṭana (Sankhu) as the capital city, suggesting that Sankhu also became a capital city in the medieval period. Statues of Viṣṇu and Lakṣmī carved on a single stone is placed atop thejalaharīthat contains this Inscription. The Statue also contains a one-line inscription that refers to the figure of Lakṣmī as Lakṣmīdevī, the wife of King Amṛta Malla, and Viṣṇu as the King’s son Abhayasiṃha. The figure of Lakṣmī appears larger than that of Viṣṇu to highlight the greater importance of the mother in comparison to the son. The portrayal of Lakṣmī as the mother of Viṣṇu in the Statue is interesting, as the Hindu mythology considers Lakṣmī as Viṣṇu’s consort. It also reveals that there was a tradition of installing statues of deities based on their iconography but referring to them as one’s family members. Another interesting object present at the Sataḥ is a stone palm exhibiting theabhayamudrā. An inscription on the back of the Palm outlines the activities to be carried out by the respectiveguthiannually.N. YogīVS 2012, p. 62 refers to this inscription asdātṛhastābhilekha(lit. the inscription on the donor’s palm)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/f6f28540-6aa7-4a61-abd1-03d8250aa25f> ;
    rdfs:comment "King Amṛta Malla had commissioned the construction of asataḥat the Sālkhā Ṭola and donated many plots of land to feed ascetics from foreign lands at the Sataḥ (N. YogīVS 2012, p. 61-62). The King got the Sataḥ made in the name of his deceased wife Lakṣmīdevī and two of his late sons Abhayasiṃha and Kṛṣṇasiṃha, along with his daughters-in-law Jayatudevī, Phikudevī, and Herādevī. A line of an inscription installed at the Sataḥ refers to Śaṅkarapaṭṭana (Sankhu) as the capital city, suggesting that Sankhu also became a capital city in the medieval period. Statues of Viṣṇu and Lakṣmī carved on a single stone is placed atop thejalaharīthat contains this Inscription. The Statue also contains a one-line inscription that refers to the figure of Lakṣmī as Lakṣmīdevī, the wife of King Amṛta Malla, and Viṣṇu as the King’s son Abhayasiṃha. The figure of Lakṣmī appears larger than that of Viṣṇu to highlight the greater importance of the mother in comparison to the son. The portrayal of Lakṣmī as the mother of Viṣṇu in the Statue is interesting, as the Hindu mythology considers Lakṣmī as Viṣṇu’s consort. It also reveals that there was a tradition of installing statues of deities based on their iconography but referring to them as one’s family members. Another interesting object present at the Sataḥ is a stone palm exhibiting theabhayamudrā. An inscription on the back of the Palm outlines the activities to be carried out by the respectiveguthiannually.N. YogīVS 2012, p. 62 refers to this inscription asdātṛhastābhilekha(lit. the inscription on the donor’s palm)."@en ;
