<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/event/fdb69fc2-0782-4930-8ef8-df57c79c1006_2> a crm:E5_Event,
        hg:HistoricalEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Reconstruction of the bāhāḥ-dyaḥchẽ (god house)"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Up until the mid-1970s, thebāhāḥ-dyaḥchẽ(god house) retained its traditional architectural elements, which included a three-story main building featuring a tile roof and a small balconied tower known as apula. The roof was supported by intricately carved struts. Around 2007 CE, thebāhāḥunderwent reconstruction, resulting in its present-day form (see 'description' section above)."@en ;
    crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of <https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/fdb69fc2-0782-4930-8ef8-df57c79c1006> ;
    rdfs:comment "Up until the mid-1970s, thebāhāḥ-dyaḥchẽ(god house) retained its traditional architectural elements, which included a three-story main building featuring a tile roof and a small balconied tower known as apula. The roof was supported by intricately carved struts. Around 2007 CE, thebāhāḥunderwent reconstruction, resulting in its present-day form (see 'description' section above)."@en ;
