<https://data.cairnepal.org/heritage/resource/monument/03320d41-173e-4308-b7a2-7f3f6c5c8126> a crm:E22_Human-Made_Object,
        hg:ArchitecturalStructure,
        hg:Temple ;
    rdfs:label "Bhīmabhakteśvara Mandira"@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "03320d41-173e-4308-b7a2-7f3f6c5c8126" ;
    dcterms:references "Sources and references Inscription identification number KAT0700_I_001 Sources N. Yogī VS 2010, pp.14-17 The inscription that documents the establishment of the temple on the second day of March in 1823 was removed from its original place and found broken into three pieces in front of the temple. It is currently (2022) leaning against the northern boundary wall. The inscription was read by Nutan Sharma in 1983 and translated by Mahesh Raj Pant in collaboration with Bishnu Raj Jha and Nutan Sharma. Of the entire 41 lines, the first nine lines are in Sanskrit, the following ten in Nepālī ( deśabhāṣa ). The final 22 lines give 85 place names of fields in Kathmandu, Bhaktapur and Thimi with a total income of 932 muri (ca. 7.85 ha of agricultural land). Details H: 160 cm, W: 98 cm Translation: “O Bhīma. Why do you afflict the mind resembling the lake Mānasarovāra. The mind is ever haunted by the fear accompanying existence. The full moon-like feet of Bhīmabhakteśvara are ever-shining. Among the five brothers [ sodareṣu – of the same uterus] to whom Satyarūpā – in the wake of the stability and prosperity ensured by the king of Gorkhā – the devoted [ sati ] wife of minister [ saciva ] Amarasiṃha, son of Vīrabhadra, gave birth to General Śrī Bhīmasena, the head of the ministers [ mantriśiromaṇi ], who is successful [ kṛtī ] in achieving noble deeds. In the year 1879 of Śrīvikrāma, when the sun is in Aquarius, in the month of Caitra, in the dark fortnight, on the day of Brahmā, a Monday, he and his wife Bhaktakumārī, who is a good devotee [ bhakti ] built a wonderful temple [ mandira ] near Kāntipura, in his own auspicious garden [ janarala bāg ] and consecrated formally Śrī Bhīmbhakteśvara, together with ornaments, a flower garden beautified by a pond. He dedicated two villages [ grama ] in addition to nine fields [ kṣetra ]. Let the person respecting the rules connected with this place obtain endless merit [ puṇya ]. So that all may be understood, the particulars are written in the local language [ deśabhāṣa ]: After building the temple with ornaments and other items, janaral Bhīmasena Thāpā installed the image of Bhīmabhakteśvara Mahādeva in his own garden near Kāntipura. He set apart tax free land [ suvā ] for the performance of daily [ nimitty ] and seasonal [ naimittika ] worship of the image. Free distribution of food (should be provided) daily to (mendicants such as) jogis and sannyāsis as well as phakīras , salaries [ ṣānagī ] and expenses [ ṣarca ] be paid to the officiating priest [ pūjāhāri ], the temple attendant [ ṭahaluva ], the singers [ bhajana garnyā, bājā bajāūnyā ] for the morning and evening performances, the sweeper [ baḍhārnyā ] as well as the members of the association [ guṭhī ] in Śālivāhanaśake saṃvat 1744, according to Vikramāditya Saṃvat 1879 on Wednesday the 17th  of the month of Phāguṇa, on the second day of the dark moon of the month of Caitra. An association [ guṭhī ] has been established with a total income of 932 muri of rice (ca. 8.5 tons) for the perpetual worship [ pūjā ] and offering of cooked rice [ bhoga ], for the distribution of 15 portions [ bhāg ] of alms, the performance of offering water to the liṅga [ jaladhārā ] in the months of Vaiśākha, Jyeṣṭha and Āṣāḍha (mid April to mid July), for the worship (of Viṣṇu) on the occasion of the two Great Elevenths [ thulā ekādaṣī ] (in July and November), for the Devatāpūjā and the feeding of the Brahmins [ brāhmaṇabhojana ] on the birthday of the general in the month of Śrāvaṇa, for the worship and great bath [ mahāsnāna ] on the day of Haritālikā (on the 3rd  day of the bright half of the moon in Bhādra), for worship on the day of the dark night of Śiva (Śivarātrī, the 14th  day of the dark half of the moon in the month of Phāguṇa), for the donation of husked rice, salt and other food items [ sidhā ] and kitchen utensils [ sarajāma ] to devotees and pilgrims on the anniversary of the temple and its deity [ varṣavaṃndhana ], the worship on the day of Śrīpañcamī (the 5th  day of the bright half of the moon in the month of Māgha/January), for altogether 25 caretakers of the association [ guṭhiyār ] – that is the head of the association, the clerk, two persons in charge of distributing alms, two attendants of the deity ([ devatā kā ṭahaluvā ], one Brāhmaṇa as priest, one Brāhmaṇa as attendant, one Brāhmaṇa who prepares the cooked rice for the deity, seven singers [ bhajanyā ], seven Kusle musicians [ bājā vajāunyā kusalā ], one sweeper [ baḍhārnyā kusalyā ], one head of the tillers [ mohināyeka ]”."@en ;
    dcterms:title "Bhīmabhakteśvara Mandira"@en ;
